course: religion-ethics
lesson: 97
title: Honest with yourself: admitting you were wrong
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Being honest with yourself means seeing the truth about your own mistakes, feelings and abilities, even when it would be more comfortable not to.
explain: Jack's team lost and he blames the ref, but deep down he knows he missed an easy pass; Jesus asked why we look at the speck of sawdust in someone else's eye and ignore the plank in our own (Matthew 7:3-5).
explain: Saying "I was wrong" costs one second and makes you wiser, like Maddie saying, "Oh, you're right, whales are mammals!"
explain: Pride gets in the way: in "The Emperor's New Clothes" nobody dared to say the emperor was wearing nothing until a child did, and "Pride goes before destruction, a haughty spirit before a fall" (Proverbs 16:18).
explain: Honest self-knowledge goes both ways: don't think you're better than you are, and don't put yourself down either; the prodigal son "came to his senses", and that was the start of his way home.
fact: "Why do you look at the speck of sawdust in your brother's eye and pay no attention to the plank in your own eye?" (Matthew 7:3-5).
fact: People tend to take credit for their wins and blame outside things for their losses; admitting your own part is how you get better.
fact: "The way of fools seems right to them, but the wise listen to advice" (Proverbs 12:15); the Greek thinker Socrates said wisdom begins with knowing what you don't know.
fact: In Aesop's fable the fox can't reach the grapes and says they're sour anyway; saying "I didn't want it anyway" can be a lie to yourself.
fact: "Do not think of yourself more highly than you ought, but rather think of yourself with sober judgment" (Romans 12:3); sober judgment means clear, honest thinking.
fact: "I praise you because I am fearfully and wonderfully made" (Psalm 139:14): being honest about yourself includes your good bits.
fact: Aristotle said the boaster claims more than is true and the falsely modest claim less; the truthful person is in the middle.
fact: Hans Christian Andersen's "The Emperor's New Clothes" (1837) shows how fear of looking foolish makes a whole crowd pretend.
fact: The prodigal son, feeding pigs, "came to his senses", went home and said, "Father, I have sinned against heaven and against you" (Luke 15:17-21).
fact: The Psalms are full of honest feelings, even complaints to God: "How long, Lord? Will you forget me for ever?" (Psalm 13:1); naming a feeling helps calm it.
fact: "Go to the ant, you sluggard; consider its ways and be wise!" (Proverbs 6:6); a sluggard is a lazy person: admit you're putting things off, and do ten minutes now.
fact: "God opposes the proud but shows favour to the humble" (James 4:6); say sorry for your part, even if the other person started it.
fact: Peter said, "Even if all fall away on account of you, I never will" (Matthew 26:33), and that night denied Jesus three times; afterwards he wept, was humble, and Jesus forgave him and gave him his work back (John 21:15-17); "Know yourself" was carved at Delphi.
fact: Slang check: "lowkey" means sort of, quietly; "salty" means annoyed; "bruv" means mate; "dead boring" means very boring.
exercise: Jack blames the ref but knows he missed an easy pass. Why is it easier to blame others? | People like to take credit for wins and blame outside things for losses | Because refs are always wrong in every match | Because it's never a player's fault | Because Jack really did play perfectly
exercise: What did Jesus say about the speck and the plank (Matthew 7:3-5)? | Notice the plank in your own eye before the speck in someone else's | Always point out other people's specks first, before looking at your own | Planks and specks are both too small to matter | Only carpenters need to worry about planks
exercise: Maddie said whales are fish, and her friend shows her the book. What is the honest response? | Oh, you're right, they're mammals! | Books can be wrong, so I'm still right | Whatever, who cares about whales | Sulk and refuse to talk about it
exercise: What did Socrates say wisdom begins with? | Knowing what you don't know | Knowing more than everybody else | Never asking any questions | Always being right in arguments
exercise: Ruby didn't get a part and says, "Acting's dead boring anyway." What fable is this like? | The fox who called the grapes he couldn't reach sour | The tortoise who won the race against the boastful hare | The ant who saved up food for winter while the grasshopper sang | The boy who cried wolf so often that nobody believed him
exercise: What is the honest thing for Ruby to do? | Admit she's disappointed, then try again or choose something else | Keep saying acting is boring until she believes it | Tell everyone the play will be rubbish | Never try out for anything again
exercise: Theo says he's "lowkey the best at maths", but his tests are in the middle. What does Romans 12:3 say? | Think of yourself with sober judgment, not more highly than you ought | Always think you're the best at everything, even when the tests say not | Never think about yourself at all | Only think about your maths marks
exercise: Ella always says "I'm rubbish at everything", but she's brilliant at drawing. Is that honest? | No; honesty about yourself includes your good bits | Yes; being modest is always honest, whatever the truth is | Yes; she should say it even more | No, because she's brilliant at everything
exercise: In Aristotle's view, where is the truthful person? | In the middle, between the boaster and the falsely modest | At the top, always claiming more | At the bottom, always claiming less | Nowhere; nobody is truthful about themselves, not even Aristotle
exercise: Why did nobody tell the emperor he was wearing nothing? | They were afraid of looking foolish | They couldn't see him from the crowd | The clothes really were invisible | The emperor told them it was a game
exercise: Who finally said the emperor had no clothes? | A child | The emperor's tailor | The queen | A soldier
exercise: What does "came to his senses" mean for the prodigal son? | He finally saw the truth about himself and went home | He woke up from a long sleep in the field with the pigs | He got his sense of smell back after a long, bad illness | He realised the pigs were hungry and needed more food
exercise: Kwame says, "Nah bruv, I ain't salty," and throws the controller. What is the honest version? | Yeah, I'm a bit salty. Rematch? | I'm not annoyed, the controller slipped | You cheated, that's why I lost | I'm never playing with you again
exercise: Tom keeps saying he'll start his project tomorrow. What helps? | Admit he's putting it off, and do ten minutes now | Make an even bigger, better plan for tomorrow instead | Wait until the night before it's due, when he works best | Ask someone else to do the whole thing for him, just this once
exercise: Lucy won't say sorry for teasing Amy because "she started it". What does James 4:6 say? | God opposes the proud but shows favour to the humble | Whoever starts it must say sorry first, and never the other one | The proud are always right in the end | Never say sorry if you didn't start it
exercise: Josh says, "I'll never forget my lines, I'm too good." What did Peter say before denying Jesus? | Even if all fall away on account of you, I never will | I am not sure I can follow you, Lord, so please do not ask me | I will deny you three times tonight | I am the least of all the disciples
test: Your mate says, "Admitting you're wrong makes you look weak." What is the best reply? | It takes one second and makes you wiser; people respect it | He's right, so never admit anything | Only teachers should admit being wrong | It's fine to admit it if nobody's watching, but never in public
test: "Shut up, whales are fish, everyone knows that," snaps Maddie. What does Proverbs 12:15 say? | The wise listen to advice | The loudest person is always right | Fools always win arguments | Never listen to your friends
test: A cartoon shows the emperor's crowd laughing at the child who said he had no clothes. What is wrong? | The child said the obvious truth everyone else was too scared to say | Nothing; the child was being rude and wrong | The emperor really was wearing clothes | The crowd were right to agree with the emperor
test: "Acting's dead boring, I never wanted it," says Ruby, crying. What would help her most? | Admitting she's disappointed, so she can try again or choose something else | Saying it more times until she believes it | Laughing at the people who got parts | Telling the teacher the play is rubbish
test: Ella says, "I'm rubbish at drawing," holding a brilliant drawing. What does Psalm 139:14 suggest? | She is wonderfully made, and honesty includes her good bits | She should hide her drawing from everyone, so she doesn't look proud | Being rubbish is part of being humble | Only grown-ups are good at drawing
test: Kwame shouts, "I ain't salty!" and kicks the sofa. What would happen if he named the feeling instead? | It would likely help him calm down | It would make him much angrier | Nothing; feelings don't change | His friend would laugh at him for ever
test: Tom says, "Tomorrow, defo," for the tenth time. What does Proverbs 6:6 say? | Go to the ant, you sluggard; consider its ways and be wise | Rest today, for tomorrow will be easier and you'll have more time | Always leave work to the last minute | Ants are lazy, so don't copy them
test: Lucy says, "She started it, so I'm not saying sorry." What is the honest step? | Say sorry for her own part, even if Amy started it | Wait for Amy to say sorry first, always, because she started it | Tease Amy back so they're even | Tell the teacher Amy started everything
test: Josh laughs, "Peter was just unlucky." What did Peter really learn? | He wasn't as strong as he boasted; afterwards he was humble and was restored | Nothing; he kept boasting afterwards | That roosters are bad luck | That he should never have followed Jesus
test: The vicar asks why the prodigal son's turning point was "coming to his senses". Which answer is best? | Being honest with himself was the first step home | He finally found the money he had lost in the far country | The pigs he looked after told him it was time to go home | His father sent a letter asking him to come back home
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the teaching recorded in Matthew 7:3-5? | One ought to attend to one's own faults before censuring the lesser faults of others | One ought to censure the faults of others before attending to one's own | One's own faults are of no consequence compared with those of others | Faults ought never to be mentioned under any circumstances
exam: Identify the principal lesson of Hans Christian Andersen's tale "The Emperor's New Clothes". | The fear of appearing foolish may induce an entire community to pretend | Tailors are invariably honest in their dealings | Children ought never to speak in the presence of rulers | Invisible garments are the most valuable of all
exam: Select the statement consistent with the view attributed to Aristotle concerning truthfulness about oneself. | The truthful person neither overstates nor understates his own merits | The truthful person consistently understates his own merits, from modesty | The truthful person consistently overstates his own merits | Truthfulness concerning oneself is impossible to attain
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the phrase "came to his senses" as applied to the prodigal son (Luke 15:17). | He attained an honest recognition of his own condition and conduct | He recovered from a physical illness affecting his sight and hearing | He discovered that his inheritance had been restored | He resolved to remain permanently in the far country
